BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Past & Present: Lost in Translation
DTSTART;TZID=America/Toronto:20261204T130000
DTEND;TZID=America/Toronto:20261204T143000
UID:past-present-lost-translation@ocadu.ca
DESCRIPTION:Past & Present: Lost in Translation\n\nPast & Present: Lost in
  Translation Friday\, December 04\, 1:00 p.m. to 2:30 p.m. Virtual Panel v
 ia YouTube Join Onsite Gallery for a thought-provoking virtual panel featu
 ring Alize Zorlutuna\, Richa Narvekar\, Dedra McDermottand others explorin
 g how artists draw on cultural traditions\, ancestral knowledge\, and dias
 poric ways of knowing to shape contemporary creative practices. The discus
 sion will focus on how visual art\, dance\, design\, storytelling\, and ma
 terial practices can reinterpret cultural knowledge while creating new rel
 ationships to memory\, identity\, land\, material culture\, and the more-t
 han-human. The panelists will share how their practices engage with inheri
 ted traditions\, not simply as forms to preserve\, but as living sources f
 or experimentation\, reclamation\, and new ways of understanding the prese
 nt. Stay involved with a live Q&A session\, moderated by Onsite Gallery’
 s Programs & Community Coordinator\, Susan Jama . ASL Interpretation will 
 be available. The panel is live-streamed via YouTube Visit Registration Pa
 ge About the Panelists Images (left to right): Alize Zorlutuna photo by Fa
 rihah Aliyah Shaw. Richa Narvekar Alize Zorlutuna is a queer interdiscipli
 nary artist\, writer\, and educator whose practice explores relationships 
 between land\, material culture\, and the more-than-human through diaspori
 c ways of knowing. Moving between Tkarónto and Anatolia has cultivated an
  attentiveness to sites of encounter\, where bodies\, geographies\, and el
 emental forces meet in ongoing processes of negotiation and co-becoming. D
 rawing on material and relational traditions from the SWANA region\, their
  work spans video\, installation\, ebru (Anatolian water marbling)\, ceram
 ics\, textiles\, and participatory practice\, bringing together diverse ma
 terials\, methods\, and geographies to consider place\, memory\, and relat
 ion. Richa Narvekar is an Architectural Designer and Visual Artist that is
  interested in the connect between Art\, Architecture\, Urban and Interior
  Design. She earned her Bachelors in Architecture at CEPT University\, Ind
 ia and was a British Council scholar to the Masters in Architecture\, Urba
 n Design program at The Bartlett School of Architecture\, London\, UK. She
  is a registered Architect under the Council of Architecture\, India and h
 olds 15+ years of experience across India and Canada. Her practice\, based
  out of Toronto\, Canada and Goa\, India is multidisciplinary\, with a foc
 us on projects in Interior Design. She has led several interior design and
  build projects as a Senior Designer for firms in the Greater Toronto Area
 . She is also involved with sustainable architectural projects and communi
 ty focused art and urban initiatives in both Canada and India. Her work is
  centered on an exploration of vernacular\, critical regionalist\, decolon
 ial and anti-capitalist approaches to material and ways of making. Images 
 (left to right): Dedra McDermott photo by Christine Do. Susan Jama . Dedra
  McDermott is a Black artist-researcher\, curator\, producer\, movement dr
 amaturg\, and cultural steward working at the intersection of choreographi
 c performance\, archival practice\, and visual culture. Following a succes
 sful career as a professional contemporary dancer across stage and screen\
 , two significant concussions in 2018 catalyzed a profound pivot toward cr
 eative leadership. Since then\, Dedra has charted a rapid\, wide-ranging t
 rajectory\, navigating a nimble transition from emerging to mid-career pra
 ctice. Grounded by a multifaceted academic foundation (BFA ’16\, MA ’2
 0\, MFA ’24)\, her cross-disciplinary work champions racialized artists 
 navigating complex creative processes. As an evolving arts leader\, she su
 pports creators re-educating the public on global Indigeneity and Black di
 asporic histories across Latin America and the Caribbean. Dedra is one of 
 the former Co-Executive Artistic Producers of DanceWorks (2023–25). Duri
 ng her tenure\, she curated and produced over 15 live presentations and ch
 oreographic development programs\, while leading access-based initiatives 
 for hard-of-hearing and d/Deaf audiences alongside community partners like
  VibraFusionLab. As a cultural steward who values the art of gathering\, D
 edra works equitably to bring a diverse public together—creating the con
 ditions for deeper cultural connection and appreciation. About the Moderat
 or Susan Jama is an art worker with over 6 years of experience leading com
 munity engagement and public arts programming with strong grassroots exper
 ience. She is the Programs and Community Coordinator at Onsite Gallery. Su
 san has worked with various institutions that approach heritage in a commu
 nity-minded manner including Toronto Ward Museum (TWM) and Black Artists
 ’ Networks in Dialogue Gallery & Cultural Centre (BAND). She graduated w
 ith a Masters of Museum Studies from University of Toronto and completed h
 er Bachelor in Psychology & History at York University. She currently serv
 es on the Museum Education Roundtable board as co-treasurer.
LOCATION:Online-Only Virtual via YouTube
URL:https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=mj5JJ3zPakc
X-SOURCE:OCAD University
END:VEVENT
