BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:2SLGBTQ+ aging with and into difference(s) | Film Screening with C
 RIP Lab and Tangled Art + Disability
DTSTART;TZID=America/Toronto:20261107T130000
DTEND;TZID=America/Toronto:20261107T143000
UID:2slgbtq-aging-and-differences-film-screening-crip-lab-and-tangled-art@
 ocadu.ca
DESCRIPTION:Presented by CRIP Lab\, University of Guelph & Tangled Arts + 
 Disabilty\n\nThe Special Edition of CRIP Lab with film screenings is here!
  We have been talking about this since our last two gatherings. Jami Mcfar
 land and Patricia Ginn would also be joining us. Very excited to see the m
 ovies with you all! This event is online only . More details about the eve
 nt are below\, along with the zoom links. Please feel free to extend the i
 nvitation to your friends 🙂 We would love to have them join us for this
  beautiful event. Saturday\, Nov 7\, 1-2:30 p.m. Presented by CRIP Lab\, U
 niversity of Guelph & Tangled Arts + Disabilty Film Screenings from a stud
 y led by Jami Mcfarland : 2SLGBTQ+ aging with and into difference(s) Story
 tellers' names and titles: Patricia Derouin’s “Not Dead Yet” (8 mins
 ) Michael Dion’s “Why Didn’t Anyone Tell Me About Aging” (6 mins\,
  39 secs) Rev. Marcie Wexler’s “Solitaire Road” (6 mins\, 20 secs) B
 ernie Thompson’s “New Beginnings” (9 mins\, 42 secs) Rita OLink’s 
 “I’m Not Going to Run\, I’m Not Going to Hide” (8 mins\, 9 secs) D
 on Turner’s “A Long Journey Into Aging” (7 mins\, 23 secs) David Dri
 nkwalter’s “My Life in the Lost and Found Department” (6 mins\, 36 s
 ecs) Dar Hansen’s “Dar’s Story” (6 mins\, 48 secs) Jo Brook’s 
 “A Fae-ry Tale” (8 mins\, 55 secs) These stories came from a SSHRC-fun
 ded study\, "(Eng)Aging Crip Rainbow Representations: 2SLGBTQ+ Disabled Ol
 der Adults Access the Arts and Create Belonging through Digital Storytelli
 ng\," which sought to create diverse representations at the nexus of 2SLGB
 TQ+ aging\, disability\, and illness. Some storytellers chose to explore d
 isability and illness directly in their stories\, while others focused on 
 other parts of their identities in their stories. Jami Mcfarland is the PI
  of this study\, but there are several other academics contributing to it:
  Carla Rice\, Nadine Changfoot\, Celeste Pang\, and Nicole Dalmer)\, and a
 n excellent RA (Andrea Keber. Funded by SSHRC\, the study was created in c
 ollaboration with Re-Vision: The Centre for Art and Social Justice at the 
 University of Guelph. Join Zoom Meeting https://us02web.zoom.us/j/86029204
 140?pwd=iYZeN6xhbyFZfbpbjONaNlYh1tduGU.1 Join and take notes https://us02w
 eb.zoom.us/j/86029204140?pwd=iYZeN6xhbyFZfbpbjONaNlYh1tduGU.1&mynotes=on M
 eeting chat link https://us02web.zoom.us/launch/jc/86029204140 Meeting ID:
  860 2920 4140 Passcode: 875139 --- One tap mobile +12532050468\,\,8602920
 4140# \,\,\,\,*875139# US +12532158782\,\,86029204140# \,\,\,\,*875139# US
  (Tacoma) --- Join by SIP • 86029204140@zoomcrc.com Join instructions ht
 tps://us02web.zoom.us/meetings/86029204140/invitations?signature=z3nWTfjmL
 OGkTs1h577Hags_C-BMtXBYFgn68V5qFPs Thank you again for everyone's support.
  If you have any queries reach out to parthshah@ocadu.ca Organizer: Parth 
 Shah (he/him) Sessional Instructor\, Faculty of Design\, OCAD University. 
 LinkedIn | Email: parthshah@ocadu.ca
URL:https://us02web.zoom.us/j/86029204140?pwd=iYZeN6xhbyFZfbpbjONaNlYh1tdu
